Title: Retire the dead retention machinery and ship the resident search line
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-10T16:24Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

The design retired four mechanisms — a decay pass, a per-type TTL table, a
corpus cap, an orphan collector — and added one: a single resident line naming
how the store is searched. None of the retirements happened, and the line does
not exist. So the repository still carries code and prose for a policy the
design abandoned, and does not carry the one line the architecture depends on.

This blocks index generation: `0910` cuts an index at N, and until the resident
line ships, a body below the cut has no documented way back. The fourth review
made that point sharply — an unlisted body is unreachable *by the design's own
§2.4* until the door exists.

Design reference: §5 (P2, and the list of what §5 removes) in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`.
Review: F7 in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-review-fable-design.md`.

## Relevant files

- `skills/dream/` — the decay pass, the `confirm` step and their tests
- `skills/memory/SKILL.md` — the TTL table
- `tests/test_resident_census.py` — the harness index budget, currently a
  ratchet stamped on a nearly empty tier rather than a decision
- `skills/dream/read-index.py` — reads the store from `MEMORY.md`, which
  inverts store and view

## Actions

1. Delete the decay pass, the `confirm` step and their tests.
2. Delete the TTL table and the 200-line cap.
3. Delete the "an entry dropped from an index is unreachable, so never unlist"
   prose wherever it survives in skill files — the architecture now unlists by
   design.
4. Restate the harness index budget as a decision about what that tier must
   carry, not a ratchet on what it currently holds. The harness and project
   indexes are separate resident channels; neither constrains the other.
5. Ship P2's resident line: one line, in the resident set, naming the search
   and how to run it.
6. Fix the `read-index.py` inversion, or file it if it is larger than it looks.

## Test

Red first: assert the resident line is present in the resident set and that the
resident set is under budget with it. Then let the suite name the callers of
what you delete — a reference list and the set of things that break are
different sets.

## Verification

- [ ] `grep -r "decay\|TTL" skills/` returns nothing load-bearing
- [ ] The resident line is present and the budget test passes with it
- [ ] Nothing in `skills/` still tells a reader that unlisting loses an entry

## Invariants

- No memory body is deleted by this ticket. It removes machinery, not content.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Decay, `confirm`, the TTL table and the 200-line cap are gone with their tests
- [ ] The harness budget is a stated decision with its reasoning
- [ ] The resident search line exists, is resident, and is covered by a test
